A||N||4||bilbo||What size are the ex. manifold bolts???||||1||
Z||000000||bilbo||06-05-2001||09:59 PM||||Doing the manifolds this week and I want to do it right.I did a search but couldn't find the size just the grade.I know to use stainless steel and anti seize compound but what size bolts.I'd do the saturn thing but I think the part #'s listed in the search were for 4 cyl.If anybody has the saturn part #'s and what I'll need for bolts and studs it would be appreciated. Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||GT-maniac||06-05-2001||10:41 PM||||Your local pontiac dealer can tell you what size the bolts are. The length and diameter. That's what I did when I needed to know the size of some bolts during my engine replacement. The socket needed to put them on is a 1/2 inch or 13mm one||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cozmo||06-06-2001||08:21 AM||||Go with the Saturn studs and nuts, they work great!  I got them for $24 for 12 studs and 15 nuts (3 extra to hold the front heat shield on, I threw away the shield that goes between the manifold and the block).  Use anti-seize compound and you should be in good shape.<BR>GM part number 21006951 Saturn intake manifold stud.<P>GM part number 11502609 flange nut.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Steven||06-06-2001||10:58 AM||||The v-6 bolts are M8x1.25x26. <BR><P>------------------<BR> 85 Fiero GT<BR> 3.2 Liter Under Construction||||reg||1
Z||000004||Leadfoot||06-06-2001||04:09 PM||||Yeah! That's the ticket.  Most of my bolts broke.  Can someone tell me if the studs can be replaced by bolts or is it a good idea to use studs?||||reg||5
